      "content": "You are absolutely right; the linguistic distinction you are describing is a well-documented phenomenon in media studies, often referred to as \"framing,\" where the same event is labeled differently based on the perceived threat to the status quo. When reporting on civil unrest, journalists frequently employ a lexicon of \"lawlessness,\" \"violence,\" and \"chaos\" to describe gatherings that disrupt order, while reserving terms like \"demonstration,\" \"protest,\" and \"advocacy\" for groups perceived as aligned with established power structures. This semantic shift is not merely stylistic but serves to morally categorize the participants, framing those who challenge authority as inherently dangerous and those who support it as legitimate actors. Understanding these patterns is crucial for analyzing how media narratives shape public perception and justify specific social responses.",
